{"id":"data-correlation","title":"Data science: correlation is not causation","subject":"Data science","label":"Data science","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":174,"duration":173.66,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":465,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"The puzzle","start":0,"end":14.045},{"id":"explain","title":"What correlation is","start":14.045,"end":58.405},{"id":"explain2","title":"Why it misleads","start":58.405,"end":98.905},{"id":"example","title":"Shoe sizes","start":98.905,"end":114.195},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":114.195,"end":126.555},{"id":"answer","title":"The answer","start":126.555,"end":135.665},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":135.665,"end":154.055},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":154.055,"end":173.66}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":2.19,"text":"I'm Kora. Here's a puzzle.","w":[0.54,0.7,1.46,1.74,1.8]},{"s":0,"a":2.49,"b":5.81,"text":"In months when more ice cream is sold, more people drown.","w":[2.58,2.76,3.14,3.36,3.66,3.86,4.1,4.22,4.92,5.14,5.44]},{"s":0,"a":6.11,"b":8.99,"text":"So does ice cream make people drown? No.","w":[6.18,6.36,6.62,6.78,7.24,7.48,7.76,8.7]},{"s":0,"a":9.29,"b":13.62,"text":"By the end of this lesson, you'll know why, and you'll spot the same trap anywhere.","w":[9.32,9.5,9.7,9.86,9.96,10.18,10.62,10.84,11.04,11.76,11.88,12.14,12.46,12.6,12.86,13.24]},{"s":1,"a":14.47,"b":17.65,"text":"Two variables are correlated when they tend to change together.","w":[14.54,14.8,15.42,15.58,16.2,16.36,16.52,16.76,16.9,17.22]},{"s":1,"a":17.95,"b":25,"text":"Positive correlation: as one goes up, the other tends to go up. More hours of study, higher test score.","w":[18,18.48,19.58,19.8,19.96,20.26,20.92,21.1,21.32,21.64,21.76,22,22.6,22.86,23.22,23.34,24,24.34,24.62]},{"s":1,"a":25.3,"b":31.96,"text":"Negative correlation: as one goes up, the other tends to go down. More phone use at night, fewer hours of sleep.","w":[25.4,25.8,26.7,26.9,27.06,27.34,27.68,27.84,28.06,28.3,28.42,28.56,29.36,29.62,29.9,30.08,30.18,30.84,31.16,31.46,31.56]},{"s":1,"a":32.26,"b":34.4,"text":"No correlation means no pattern at all.","w":[32.38,32.58,33.22,33.56,33.76,34.14,34.24]},{"s":1,"a":34.7,"b":40.76,"text":"A scatter plot shows it: dots rising to the right, falling to the right, or a shapeless cloud.","w":[34.78,34.86,35.26,35.58,35.92,36.5,36.88,37.3,37.42,37.56,38.26,38.68,38.8,38.94,39.56,39.72,39.86,40.34]},{"s":1,"a":41.06,"b":45.24,"text":"The strength is measured by the correlation coefficient, written as the letter r.","w":[41.08,41.24,41.74,41.9,42.22,42.36,42.48,43.06,43.96,44.36,44.48,44.6,45.08]},{"s":1,"a":45.54,"b":54.36,"text":"It runs from minus one, a perfect negative straight line, through zero, no straight line, to plus one, perfect positive.","w":[45.6,45.72,46.04,46.26,46.8,47.56,47.72,48.28,48.66,48.98,49.68,50,50.92,51.16,51.46,52.14,52.32,52.78,53.44,53.86]},{"s":1,"a":54.66,"b":57.98,"text":"Correlation describes a pattern. That is all it describes.","w":[54.7,55.3,55.74,55.8,56.68,56.88,57.02,57.2,57.28]},{"s":2,"a":58.83,"b":64.2,"text":"But here is the warning. Correlation does not prove causation, that one thing causes the other.","w":[58.86,59.02,59.24,59.36,59.46,60.34,60.96,61.16,61.36,61.64,62.72,63.04,63.22,63.46,63.84,64]},{"s":2,"a":64.5,"b":66.38,"text":"There are three other explanations.","w":[64.52,64.72,64.92,65.36,65.6]},{"s":2,"a":66.68,"b":75.45,"text":"One, a third variable causes both. We call it a confounder. Ice cream sales and drownings rise together because hot weather increases both.","w":[66.86,67.46,67.62,67.92,68.44,68.86,69.62,69.78,70.02,70.16,70.24,71.48,71.68,72,72.4,72.52,72.98,73.3,73.74,74.1,74.36,74.68,75.22]},{"s":2,"a":75.75,"b":78.19,"text":"Two, the cause runs the other way round.","w":[75.8,76.58,76.7,77.04,77.3,77.44,77.62,77.8]},{"s":2,"a":78.49,"b":82.89,"text":"Three, coincidence. It is easy to find when you compare many things.","w":[78.54,79.22,80.42,80.54,80.74,80.98,81.12,81.46,81.6,81.7,82.14,82.44]},{"s":2,"a":83.19,"b":90.84,"text":"To show cause, you need a fair test: an experiment where only one thing is changed, ideally with people put into groups at random.","w":[83.2,83.36,83.64,84.3,84.44,84.62,84.72,84.96,85.8,85.92,86.54,86.82,87.16,87.3,87.52,87.66,88.52,88.98,89.16,89.46,89.64,89.86,90.34,90.46]},{"s":2,"a":91.14,"b":93.49,"text":"That is called a randomised controlled trial.","w":[91.18,91.4,91.52,91.8,91.92,92.56,93.08]},{"s":2,"a":93.79,"b":98.48,"text":"Or you can study the other explanations very carefully, and rule them out one by one.","w":[93.88,94.02,94.14,94.32,94.62,94.8,95.02,95.72,96,96.9,97.08,97.32,97.5,97.78,97.96,98.24]},{"s":3,"a":99.33,"b":102.73,"text":"A town finds that children with bigger shoe sizes read better.","w":[99.38,99.48,99.9,100.24,100.44,100.86,101.12,101.48,101.72,102.2,102.42]},{"s":3,"a":103.03,"b":104.99,"text":"Do shoes teach reading? No.","w":[103.03,103.26,103.62,103.92,104.72]},{"s":3,"a":105.29,"b":109.61,"text":"Age is the confounder. Older children have bigger feet and more schooling.","w":[105.4,105.74,105.86,105.98,107.08,107.38,107.8,108.06,108.4,108.78,108.92,109.16]},{"s":3,"a":109.91,"b":113.77,"text":"So the link is real, but the cause is age, not shoes.","w":[109.98,110.14,110.26,110.52,110.68,111.48,111.62,111.74,112.08,112.36,113,113.28]},{"s":4,"a":114.62,"b":119.92,"text":"Quick check. A study finds that people who carry lighters are more likely to get lung cancer.","w":[114.66,114.94,115.8,115.9,116.3,116.7,116.88,117.18,117.34,117.7,118.2,118.36,118.6,118.98,119.06,119.28,119.5]},{"s":4,"a":120.22,"b":123.3,"text":"Do lighters cause cancer? What is the hidden variable?","w":[120.26,120.46,120.9,121.2,122.08,122.26,122.38,122.5,122.78]},{"s":4,"a":123.75,"b":126.13,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[123.8,124.12,124.36,124.5,124.78,125.52,125.74]},{"s":5,"a":126.98,"b":128.74,"text":"No. Smoking is the confounder.","w":[127.02,127.56,127.98,128.1,128.2]},{"s":5,"a":129.04,"b":133.27,"text":"People who carry lighters are more likely to smoke, and smoking causes cancer.","w":[129.08,129.42,129.58,129.9,130.32,130.46,130.66,130.98,131.08,131.74,131.94,132.42,132.8]},{"s":5,"a":133.57,"b":135.24,"text":"The lighters themselves do nothing.","w":[133.6,133.76,134.14,134.7,134.9]},{"s":6,"a":136.09,"b":140.7,"text":"First, saying one thing causes another when the data only shows the two are linked.","w":[136.18,136.92,137.36,137.52,137.78,138.2,138.86,139,139.1,139.4,139.64,139.94,140.06,140.24,140.38]},{"s":6,"a":141,"b":144.6,"text":"Second, ignoring a third variable, like age or weather.","w":[141.1,141.78,142.26,142.38,142.68,143.34,143.78,144.12,144.24]},{"s":6,"a":144.9,"b":147.61,"text":"Third, trusting a pattern from a few points.","w":[144.92,145.74,146.18,146.26,146.66,146.84,146.92,147.14]},{"s":6,"a":147.91,"b":153.63,"text":"And remember, r near zero means no straight line relationship, not no relationship of any kind.","w":[147.94,148.08,148.96,149.1,149.36,149.76,150.1,150.32,150.64,150.88,151.96,152.22,152.38,153.02,153.14,153.32]},{"s":7,"a":154.48,"b":157.72,"text":"Recap. Correlation means two variables move together.","w":[154.54,155.2,155.82,156.16,156.46,157.06,157.3]},{"s":7,"a":158.02,"b":161.28,"text":"The coefficient r runs from minus one to plus one.","w":[158.02,158.2,159.06,159.24,159.56,159.76,160.24,160.58,160.76,161.14]},{"s":7,"a":161.58,"b":164.76,"text":"A fair test, not a link, is what shows cause.","w":[161.64,161.8,162.08,162.9,163.12,163.2,163.62,163.74,163.96,164.28]},{"s":7,"a":165.06,"b":168.87,"text":"Top tip: when you see a link, ask what else could explain it.","w":[165.08,165.44,166.04,166.2,166.36,166.54,166.62,167.34,167.6,167.9,168.1,168.28,168.66]},{"s":7,"a":169.17,"b":173.06,"text":"Keep practising with me at Knowzone, and these ideas will stay with you.","w":[169.22,169.46,170,170.18,170.38,170.48,171.48,171.6,171.88,172.26,172.46,172.7,172.86]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Puzzle: ice cream & drowning","items":[{"at":2.39,"kind":"text","text":"More ice cream sales, more drownings"},{"at":6.01,"kind":"text","text":"Does ice cream cause drowning? No."},{"at":9.19,"kind":"text","text":"Spot the same trap anywhere."}]},{"at":14.22,"heading":"Correlation","items":[{"at":14.37,"kind":"term","text":"Correlation","detail":"Two variables tend to change together"},{"at":17.85,"kind":"term","text":"Positive correlation","detail":"As one goes up, the other tends to go up"},{"at":25.2,"kind":"term","text":"Negative correlation","detail":"As one goes up, the other tends to go down"},{"at":32.16,"kind":"text","text":"No correlation: no pattern at all"},{"at":34.6,"kind":"text","text":"Scatter plot: rising, falling, or cloud"}]},{"at":40.81,"heading":"Correlation coefficient r","items":[{"at":40.96,"kind":"term","text":"Correlation coefficient r","detail":"Measures strength of correlation"},{"at":45.44,"kind":"formula","text":"−1 ≤ r ≤ +1"},{"at":45.44,"kind":"image","text":"Scatter plots; the number above each one is r","src":"scatter.webp","credit":"DenisBoigelot · CC0 · Wikimedia Commons"},{"at":54.56,"kind":"text","text":"Correlation describes a pattern only"}]},{"at":58.58,"heading":"Correlation ≠ causation","items":[{"at":58.73,"kind":"text","text":"Correlation does not prove causation"},{"at":64.4,"kind":"text","text":"Three other explanations"},{"at":66.58,"kind":"term","text":"Confounder","detail":"Third variable causes both (e.g. hot weather)"},{"at":75.65,"kind":"text","text":"Reverse causation: cause runs other way"},{"at":78.39,"kind":"text","text":"Coincidence: easy with many comparisons"}]},{"at":82.94,"heading":"Showing cause","items":[{"at":83.09,"kind":"text","text":"Fair test: change only one thing"},{"at":91.04,"kind":"term","text":"Randomised controlled trial","detail":"Experiment with random groups"},{"at":93.69,"kind":"text","text":"Or rule out other explanations one by one"}]},{"at":99.08,"heading":"Example: shoe size & reading","items":[{"at":99.23,"kind":"text","text":"Bigger shoes, better reading"},{"at":102.93,"kind":"text","text":"Do shoes teach reading? No."},{"at":105.19,"kind":"term","text":"Confounder: age","detail":"Older children: bigger feet, more schooling"},{"at":109.81,"kind":"text","text":"Link is real; cause is age, not shoes"}]},{"at":114.19,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":114.19,"kind":"question","text":"People who carry lighters are more likely to get lung cancer. Do lighters cause cancer? What is the hidden variable?"}]},{"at":126.73,"heading":"Answer: hidden variable","items":[{"at":126.88,"kind":"text","text":"No: smoking is the confounder"},{"at":128.94,"kind":"text","text":"Smokers carry lighters; smoking causes cancer"},{"at":133.47,"kind":"text","text":"Lighters themselves do nothing"}]},{"at":135.84,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":135.99,"kind":"wrong","text":"Link means one thing causes the other"},{"at":140.9,"kind":"wrong","text":"Ignore a third variable"},{"at":144.8,"kind":"wrong","text":"Trust a pattern from a few points"},{"at":147.81,"kind":"wrong","text":"r near 0 means no relationship at all"}]},{"at":154.23,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":154.38,"kind":"text","text":"Correlation: two variables move together"},{"at":157.92,"kind":"text","text":"r runs from −1 to +1"},{"at":161.48,"kind":"text","text":"Fair test shows cause, not a link"},{"at":164.96,"kind":"tip","text":"When you see a link, ask what else could explain it."},{"at":169.07,"kind":"text","text":"Practise at Knowzone"}]}],"check":{"at":126.38,"seconds":10,"question":"People who carry lighters are more likely to get lung cancer. Do lighters cause cancer? What is the hidden variable?","answer":"No. Smoking is the confounder: smokers carry lighters, and smoking causes cancer."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[{"title":"Correlation examples2.svg","author":"DenisBoigelot, original uploader was Imagecreator","licence":"CC0","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Correlation_examples2.svg"}],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}